      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Although interest in sphincter-sparing treatments for anal fistulas is increasing, few large prospective studies of these approaches have been conducted."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "The study assessed outcomes after implantation of a synthetic bioabsorbable anal fistula plug."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "A prospective, multicenter investigation was performed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "The study was conducted at 11 colon and rectal centers."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "Ninety-three patients (71 men; mean age, 47 years) with complex cryptoglandular transsphincteric anal fistulas were enrolled. Exclusion criteria included Crohn's disease, an active infection, a multitract fistula, and an immunocompromised status."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTION",
          "content": "Draining setons were used at the surgeon's discretion. Patients had follow-up evaluations at 1, 3, 6, and 12 months postoperatively."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "The primary end point was healing of the fistula, defined as drainage cessation plus closure of the external opening, at 6 and 12 months. Secondary end points were fecal continence, duration of drainage from the fistula, pain, and adverse events during follow-up."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Thirteen patients were lost to follow-up and 21 were withdrawn, primarily to undergo an alternative treatment. The fistula healing rates at 6 and 12 months were 41% (95% CI, 30%-52%; total n = 74) and 49% (95% CI, 38%-61%; total n = 73). Half the patients in whom a previous treatment failed had healing. By 6 months, the mean Wexner score had improved significantly (p = 0.0003). By 12 months, 93% of patients had no or minimal pain. Adverse events included 11 infections/abscesses, 2 new fistulas, and 8 total and 5 partial plug extrusions. The fistula healed in 3 patients with a partial extrusion."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "LIMITATIONS",
          "content": "The study was nonrandomized and had relatively high rates of loss to follow-up."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Implantation of a synthetic bioabsorbable fistula plug is a reasonably efficacious treatment for complex transsphincteric anal fistulas, especially given the simplicity and low morbidity of the procedure."
        }
      ]},
